WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.302 --> 00:00:05.039
Two members of the submarine’s crew helped us to dress for our underwater walk.

2
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:11.207
Our diving suits were made of heavy rubber and constructed to resist the great pressure of the water.

3
00:00:11.208 --> 00:00:17.691
Each suit consisted of a jacket and trousers joined together with bands of copper crossing the chest.

4
00:00:17.692 --> 00:00:21.858
The sleeves ended in gloves that allowed the fingers to move easily.

5
00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:26.533
The trousers were finished off with thick boots weighted with heavy lead.

6
00:00:26.534 --> 00:00:31.446
Captain Nemo, one of his crew, Conseil, and I were soon dressed.

7
00:00:31.447 --> 00:00:37.446
The upper part of the diving suit ended in a copper collar upon which was screwed a metal helmet.

8
00:00:37.447 --> 00:00:42.850
Three holes, protected by thick glass, allowed us to see in all directions.

9
00:00:42.851 --> 00:00:48.430
I was relieved to find that I could breathe with ease once the apparatus was turned on.

10
00:00:48.431 --> 00:00:52.271
Our diving suits were so heavy that it was difficult to walk.

11
00:00:52.272 --> 00:00:56.859
Fortunately we only had to take a few steps into another small room.

12
00:00:56.860 --> 00:01:01.930
Then the door closed and we were wrapped in profound darkness.

13
00:01:01.931 --> 00:01:11.327
After a few minutes, I heard a loud hissing and felt cold rising from my feet to my chest. Evidently water was entering the room.

14
00:01:11.328 --> 00:01:18.161
When the room was full of water, another door opened in the side of the submarine and we stepped onto the sea bottom!

15
00:01:18.162 --> 00:01:27.811
How can I explain what it was like to walk underwater? Mere words are insufficient. I was astonished by how bright it was.

16
00:01:27.812 --> 00:01:34.557
Would you believe me if I said that at a depth of thirty feet I could see as if in broad daylight?

17
00:01:34.558 --> 00:01:47.381
The sun’s rays touched flowers, rocks, plants, and shells, providing a perfect kaleidoscope of colors: red, green, yellow, orange, purple, and blue.

18
00:01:47.382 --> 00:01:53.056
I wanted to share my amazement with Conseil, but underwater there was no way to communicate with him. Soon

19
00:01:53.057 --> 00:01:58.040
the nature of the soil changed from sand to slimy mud. Then

20
00:01:58.041 --> 00:02:04.199
we traveled over a plain of algae that was as soft as any carpet woven by man. After

21
00:02:04.200 --> 00:02:13.191
a while the ground began to slope downward. The colors were no longer as brilliant, but we had no need of our lights yet. When

22
00:02:13.192 --> 00:02:19.024
we reached a depth of about three hundred feet, Captain Nemo signaled for us to stop. He

23
00:02:19.025 --> 00:02:23.104
pointed to a dark mass looming in the shadows. "This

24
00:02:23.105 --> 00:02:28.342
must be the forest of Crespo," I thought and I was right. As

25
00:02:28.343 --> 00:02:34.254
the captain had said, the forest was underwater. There was no sign of an island. Captain

26
00:02:34.255 --> 00:02:37.675
Nemo regarded this forest as his own. And

27
00:02:37.676 --> 00:02:41.850
who would have challenged his possession of this underwater real estate? What

28
00:02:41.851 --> 00:02:47.659
other pioneer would come, hatchet in hand, to cut down these dark masses of plant life? The

29
00:02:47.660 --> 00:02:51.921
forest was composed of large plants that resembled trees. I

30
00:02:51.922 --> 00:02:58.151
was struck by the unusual position of their branches, something that I had never observed before. No

31
00:02:58.152 --> 00:03:04.072
herb that carpeted the ground or branch that adorned a tree extended horizontally. They

32
00:03:04.073 --> 00:03:09.238
all stretched up to the surface of the ocean as straight as iron bars. I

33
00:03:09.239 --> 00:03:15.135
soon grew used to this strange fact as well as to the growing darkness that surrounded us. The

34
00:03:15.136 --> 00:03:23.135
soil of the forest was covered with sharp rocks that were difficult to avoid, and it was hard to tell the plants from the animals. It

35
00:03:23.136 --> 00:03:32.373
was still impossible to speak and be heard, so I put my great copper head next to Conseil’s. His eyes were shining with delight. After

36
00:03:32.374 --> 00:03:38.206
a couple hours of walking underwater, I was surprised that I wasn’t terribly hungry. Instead

37
00:03:38.207 --> 00:03:46.777
I felt an overwhelming desire to sleep, something that happens to all divers. We all stretched out for a nap. I

38
00:03:46.778 --> 00:03:52.531
don’t know how long I stayed asleep, but when I awoke, Captain Nemo had already risen. I

39
00:03:52.532 --> 00:03:57.778
was starting to stretch my limbs when something startled me and I jumped to my feet. A

40
00:03:57.779 --> 00:04:07.682
few steps away a giant sea spider, thirty-eight inches high, was watching me and was ready to spring. Although

41
00:04:07.683 --> 00:04:13.682
my diving suit was thick enough to protect me from this animal, I couldn’t help shuddering with horror. At

42
00:04:13.683 --> 00:04:21.166
this moment Conseil and the other sailor from the Nautilus awoke, and Captain Nemo pointed out the hideous crustacean. He

43
00:04:21.167 --> 00:04:25.849
knocked the creature over with his gun, and I saw its horrible claws. This

44
00:04:25.850 --> 00:04:33.587
incident made me aware that we might encounter even more fearful animals—creatures whose attacks might penetrate my diving suit. I

45
00:04:33.588 --> 00:04:37.587
hadn’t thought of this before, but I resolved to be on my guard. I

46
00:04:37.588 --> 00:04:42.992
was sure that our resting spot would mark the end of our walk, but Captain Nemo pressed on. We

47
00:04:42.993 --> 00:04:50.317
continued going downhill until we reached a depth of 450 feet and it got very dark. I

48
00:04:50.318 --> 00:04:58.722
had no instruments to measure this depth, but I knew that the sun’s rays couldn’t penetrate even the clearest water at 450 feet. We

49
00:04:58.723 --> 00:05:02.055
turned on our lights and could see ahead of us. Captain

50
00:05:02.056 --> 00:05:08.888
Nemo kept plunging into the depths of the forest, whose trees were getting scarcer at every step. I

51
00:05:08.889 --> 00:05:13.460
was sure that our lights would draw some deep-sea dweller from its dark bed. But

52
00:05:13.461 --> 00:05:17.801
if sea creatures did approach us, they kept a respectful distance. Several

53
00:05:17.802 --> 00:05:23.872
times Captain Nemo raised his gun to his shoulder, but then dropped it and walked on. At last

54
00:05:23.873 --> 00:05:30.689
this marvelous excursion came to an end when we reached a wall of solid rock. There was

55
00:05:30.690 --> 00:05:38.610
no way to climb it. We had reached the base of the island of Crespo, and Captain Nemo would not venture onto land. We returned

56
00:05:38.611 --> 00:05:42.356
home by a different, steeper route. As we

57
00:05:42.357 --> 00:05:49.753
reached shallower waters, the captain shot a sea otter and his companion brought down a bird that was hovering above the waves. Off in

58
00:05:49.754 --> 00:05:55.927
the distance I spotted the bright light from the Nautilus ; we would be onboard in less than twenty minutes. I

59
00:05:55.928 --> 00:06:00.102
could tell that my air supply was beginning to run low on oxygen. Suddenly

60
00:06:00.103 --> 00:06:07.253
Captain Nemo rushed toward me and pushed me to the ground. His companion did the same thing to Conseil. I

61
00:06:07.254 --> 00:06:14.507
didn’t know what to think of this unexpected attack, but I was reassured by seeing the captain lie down beside me and remain motionless. I

62
00:06:14.508 --> 00:06:22.665
was stretched on the ground just under the shelter of an algae bush when I saw an enormous mass glide overhead. My

63
00:06:22.666 --> 00:06:28.911
blood froze in my veins as I recognized two giant sharks passing over us. They

64
00:06:28.912 --> 00:06:35.887
were blue sharks—an especially fierce kind of shark that could crush a man with their iron jaws! Happily

65
00:06:35.888 --> 00:06:39.808
for us, these hungry creatures couldn’t see well. They

66
00:06:39.809 --> 00:06:44.562
passed without spotting us even though they brushed us with their fins. By

67
00:06:44.563 --> 00:06:50.451
some miracle, we managed to escape a danger greater than meeting a tiger face-to-face! In

68
00:06:50.452 --> 00:06:53.713
a few minutes, we were back at the Nautilus .

69
00:06:53.714 --> 00:06:58.030
I don’t know whether Conseil had classified the sharks as they swam over us.

70
00:06:58.031 --> 00:07:05.452
All I know is that I had observed them more as a potential victim than as a naturalist.

